<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.1.4: Using Power Properties for Exponents</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01242</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.1.4: Using Power Properties for Exponents</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d46c6fee-388b-41c2-a441-40a95b3db747</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>Work with a partner to complete the activity. Discuss any differences in the simplified results you find. Make sure you both understand each step you take to simplify.</para>
<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">Quotient to a Negative Power Property</para>
  <hr/>
  <para>If \(a\) and \(b\) are real numbers, \(a\neq0\), \(b\neq0\), and \(n\) is an integer, then</para>
  <para>\((\frac{a}{b})^{-n}=(\frac{b}{a})^n\)</para>
</note>

<para>Complete problems 1 - 3 using the product property for exponents. Double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge.</para>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((\frac{3}{7})^{-5}\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>\((\frac{7}{3})^5 = (\frac{7^5}{3^5}) = \frac{16,807}{243}\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((-\frac{y}{z})^{-7}\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\((-\frac{z}{y})^7 = -\frac{z^7}{y^7}\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((\frac{g}{h})^{-6}\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\((\frac{h}{g})^6 =\frac{ h^6}{g^6}\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">Power Property for Exponents</para>
  <hr/>
  <para>If \(a\) is a real number and \(m\) and \(n\) are integers, then</para>
  <para>\((a^m)^n=a^{m \cdot n}\)</para>
</note>

<para>Complete problems 4 - 6 using the power property for exponents. Double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge.</para>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((4^2)^5\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(4^{10} = 1,048,576\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((n^3)^7\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(n^{21}\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((2^3)^2(d^4)^3\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\((2^6)(d^{12}) = 64d^{12}\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">Product to a Power Property for Exponents</para>
  <hr/>
  <para>If \(a\) and \(b\) are real numbers and \(m\) is a whole number, then</para>
  <para>\((ab)^m=a^mb^m\)</para>
</note>

<para>Complete problems 7 - 9 using the product to a power property for exponents. Double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge.</para>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((-2tv)^7\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\((-2)^7t^7v^7 = -128t^7v^7\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((4pr^2)^5\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(4^5p^5r^{10} = 1,024p^5r^{10}\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((3h^3)^{-2}\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\((\frac{1}{3h^3})^2 = \frac{1}{3^2h^6} = \frac{1}{9h^6}\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<para>Choose one example above and explain each step to your partner. Listen as your partner does the same for you.</para></section>
</content>
